WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.049
Andy Rydzewski, Cinematographer: I know on
bigger shows that happens a lot like the CSI's

00:00:04.049 --> 00:00:05.190
of the world.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:09.300
Directors come in and out and they don't get
a lot of time with their cinematographers

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.980
and that's got to be scary.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.750
That has to be very challenging.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:17.600
Film Courage: How many times do you read a
screenplay before you step on set?

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.529
Andy: That's a really good question.

00:00:21.529 --> 00:00:29.110
It's funny, whenever I start a new project
and I get this the script I always think I'm

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:30.640
going to read it a million times.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:37.430
I think I'm going to read it every night and
inevitably other things come my way and I

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:43.730
end up not reading it as many times as I want
but that said I read scenes.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:46.380
I won't read it beginning to end that many
times.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:54.700
A few times certainly right at the beginning
but then it really very quickly turns into

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:55.800
a puzzle.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:01.719
It's almost like you're given the puzzle all
as a finished piece and then almost immediately

00:01:01.719 --> 00:01:05.959
you break it up and then it's little puzzle
pieces and you're like okay this one we're

00:01:05.959 --> 00:01:12.969
gonna do on day 17 this one and you almost
separate it out and so it and so i'll read

00:01:12.969 --> 00:01:21.960
the scenes a number of times but as a whole
I might not revisit it again it's very it's

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:30.469
strange but also in tv there are rewrites
happening all the time so you have to reread

00:01:30.469 --> 00:01:37.099
because there are new drafts coming and sometimes
this has not happened much but it has happened

00:01:37.099 --> 00:01:44.810
where there's a rewrite that happens and you're
in week nine and there's a rewrite and I will

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:51.079
have missed an email or something and I won't
realize and we'll be on set and like oh this

00:01:51.079 --> 00:01:56.090
is where they walk out to the kitchen you're
like no no no they go to sleep I remember

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:01.539
this and like no no in the rewrite now it
goes you're like oh no like I didn't and so

00:02:01.539 --> 00:02:07.219
you scramble and it sucks when that happens
but it does happen tv and different shows

00:02:07.219 --> 00:02:14.230
some shows are rewriting as you're on set
I mean pen 15 has a lot of improv which is

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:19.680
a little different but they will improvise
and as we're doing it they'll we'll realize

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.890
oh this affects a scene that we have to shoot
next week or this affects a scene we've already

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:33.310
shot so the whole thing does need to be in
my head pretty well so it's I guess the answer

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:40.109
is I never read it enough I always think I'm
going to read it more but it's always close

00:02:40.109 --> 00:02:46.420
I'm always if I have a minute especially on
the day the morning of I reread the scenes

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:51.090
that we're shooting for the day for sure to
refresh but then I also try to look at for

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:55.500
every scene that you're shooting you look
to either side of it like where did we just

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:59.819
come out of and then what are we going back
into because that affects your first and last

00:02:59.819 --> 00:03:04.629
shot in a sequence too because you want it
to you don't want it to be jarring when it

00:03:04.629 --> 00:03:10.609
cuts to the next thing so you're trying to
hold a lot in your mind do you take any notes

00:03:10.609 --> 00:03:15.689
or storyboard when you're going through a
script yeah I don't storyboard when I'm reading

00:03:15.689 --> 00:03:21.280
a script but I do take notes I take a lot
of notes on but it's it's especially at the

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:26.690
beginning it's very loose it it's whatever
occurs to me like sometimes that's an actual

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:33.190
shot i'll write down oh slow zoom in or big
wide or what whatever whatever that is or

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:42.299
get a close-up of the scissors anything whatever
that for what if it strikes me but often and

00:03:42.299 --> 00:03:47.430
sometimes it's it's something a little broader
like oh handheld maybe this is a handheld

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:54.420
moment or maybe this is static or maybe some
it's rare at the beginning but sometimes like

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:59.489
a color might occur to me something really
i'll let myself jot down anything and then

00:03:59.489 --> 00:04:07.180
i'll let future me decide if that's relevant
or not but I do take notes I don't storyboard

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:13.180
and I have rarely worked with directors who
storyboard unless it's a commercial because

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:19.640
it's so much smaller it's much easier or if
there's a difficult sequence difficult can

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:28.460
be maybe it's an action sequence or it's just
like let's say four people in a car can get

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:34.160
very confusing and so sometimes i'll have
directors who will storyboard because they

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:39.669
want to make sure like oh this and like that
everything is kind of intentional but usually

00:04:39.669 --> 00:04:46.759
it's shot lists and so it'll be a list of
like wide medium close-up on her we need to

00:04:46.759 --> 00:04:53.910
zoom for this moment and it's a list and that's
kind of your guide and your map and very often

00:04:53.910 --> 00:05:00.810
that all changes on the day maybe because
an actor goes a certain way or maybe because

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:08.250
you have a new idea but having that plan goes
a long way and even if you never use a single

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:13.120
shot it means because there are going to be
days where you're not feeling it where you're

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:17.819
not like you're just whatever the actors are
out of it or the directors out of it or I'm

00:05:17.819 --> 00:05:23.180
out of it and then you have something to fall
back on that will still work like this is

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:28.580
still going to this is this is still a recipe
that in the end you have a scene so I think

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:33.129
it's really important to have that really
important to plan and talk that out but then

00:05:33.129 --> 00:05:40.440
on the day always being ready for something
better and or if just something changes dramatically

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:48.420
you lose a location or whatever I think being
able to to improvise is really valuable do

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:53.419
you come up with your own shot list or do
you always leave that up to the director generally

00:05:53.419 --> 00:06:00.560
the director brings a shot list very often
we do that together that's the ideal is that

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:07.780
a director and I the really the best what
I find is is the best way it's just generally

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:14.509
not practical is to be able to go with the
director to a location and be up on your feet

00:06:14.509 --> 00:06:21.210
and actually like walk around use the phone
as a little like take pictures we'll do that

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:27.590
that is that's the ideal rarely do you have
time to go ahead of time with a director to

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:34.229
a location usually you have to pay for locations
whenever you're there so but yeah generally

00:06:34.229 --> 00:06:39.420
it's with a director you sit through each
scene and you talk it out and you're kind

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:47.319
of imagining how actors might move in the
space and what moments do you need but sometimes

00:06:47.319 --> 00:06:52.050
a director will just come with their own shot
list that they've done on their own and then

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:58.250
I look through it we talk through it maybe
I have suggestions really where I think one

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:06.009
of the strengths that I have is on the day
because you're always battling time is watching

00:07:06.009 --> 00:07:13.740
the directors and actors walk through a scene
when I can see it up on its feet I can often

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:20.430
figure out a way to combine two or three shots
into one like oh if she starts here and then

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:24.939
she walks to there and I slide the camera
here then we'll actually get both of those

00:07:24.939 --> 00:07:32.689
shots on your list like that's that's something
that I think is very helpful for time but

00:07:32.689 --> 00:07:39.850
also trying to cut down on the number of shots
just for for to try and control the edit a

00:07:39.850 --> 00:07:46.340
little bit to tell the story again with intention
is that it's like well instead of just get

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:49.850
putting the camera here and then putting it
here and putting it here and putting it here

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:55.680
it's like maybe we can travel maybe this actor's
move can pull the camera and now you're in

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:02.800
a new setup that gets you this other thing
that you you wanted so but it's it's a combination

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:06.689
for sure and different directors some are
better at shot listings some are worse some

00:08:06.689 --> 00:08:14.560
hate it some love it and so I have to be kind
of amenable to all of that how much do you

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:22.449
plan out the day before your shoot the ideal
is that every day is very well planned out

00:08:22.449 --> 00:08:29.919
and that doesn't mean it won't change because
it will change but the ideal is that way now

00:08:29.919 --> 00:08:37.100
that said sometimes working on series you
don't get time to prep with the directors

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:43.880
some directors come in and let's say they're
doing episodes four and five and they you've

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:49.070
hopefully met them but even sometimes you're
meeting them on the day and they're coming

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:56.010
in and they've done their own prep that's
separate from you and you try to do your best

00:08:56.010 --> 00:09:02.090
to to work with them and I know on bigger
shows that happens a lot like the CSI's of

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:07.210
the world directors come in and out and they
don't get a lot of time with their cinematographers

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:12.250
and that's that's got to be scary that has
to be very challenging I've been lucky enough

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:21.220
that that's rare for me usually I get some
time with them but very often they will a

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:28.240
director will come in with a plan that I have
never seen before and then I will depending

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:35.600
on their comfort level I will try and make
that fit like our location a little more or

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:39.430
the feel of the show because I've been shooting
it for a while so I'm like oh normally we

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:48.120
won't zoom here or whatever that is so it's
yeah it varies and it's it's and then there

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:53.400
are I have worked with directors where there
we just have a good rhythm and we just kind

00:09:53.400 --> 00:10:00.880
of wing it like we we show up on the day and
I'm pretty good at if I have actors and a

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:10.710
director and all that I can figure out what
shots create a scene for sure so but that's

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:15.540
taken years of shooting shows for sure but
at this point I feel pretty comfortable with

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:16.540
that.

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:16.547
What did you like about this video?

